---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About & Background"
  description="Senior Frontend Engineer and UI Architect with 15+ years of experience across enterprise web architecture, frontend platforms, and applied AI systems."
  schemaType="person"
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl">
    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="pb-8 border-b border-[var(--border-subtle)] mb-8">
      <div class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-[var(--accent)] font-semibold mb-2">
        Biography & Perspective
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-4">
        Orielsy Diaz
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-relaxed">
        Senior Frontend Engineer and UI Architect with 15+ years of experience building scalable web applications, enterprise platforms, and high-profile digital experiences.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Main Content Sections -->
    <div class="space-y-12">
      
      <!-- Core Background & Positioning -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--text-muted)] font-semibold mb-4">
          Core Background & Positioning
        </h2>
        <div class="prose max-w-none">
          <p>
            My deepest professional background is frontend engineering and UI architecture: building enterprise applications, establishing reusable component and frontend patterns, modernizing long-lived platforms, and turning complex automation and backend functionality into interfaces people can actually use.
          </p>
          <p>
            More recently, my work has expanded into <strong>applied AI and context engineering</strong>: exploring how generative models, repository-specific coding-agent knowledge systems, Model Context Protocol (MCP), and local runtimes (WebLLM, Ollama) can be integrated directly into real developer workflows and product interfaces.
          </p>
          <p>
            I'm not an ML researcher training models. My interest is in what happens one layer above that: how emerging capabilities can be turned into useful products, developer systems, and interfaces before an established playbook exists.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- How I Think -->
      <section class="p-6 rounded-lg border border-[var(--border-subtle)] bg-[var(--bg-secondary)]/30">
        <h2 class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--text-muted)] font-semibold mb-3">
          How I Think
        </h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-xs sm:text-sm text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-relaxed">
          <div>
            <strong class="text-[var(--text-primary)] block mb-1">1. The Right Context at the Right Time</strong>
            <span>More information is not automatically better. Whether the consumer is an autonomous coding agent or a person navigating a complex product, I tend to think in terms of filtering and surfacing the information or capability useful at that particular moment rather than presenting everything at once.</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong class="text-[var(--text-primary)] block mb-1">2. AI as a Capability, Not Just a Chatbot</strong>
            <span>Chat is only one narrow modality. Structured extraction, inline classification, semantic filtering, contextual actions, and adaptive guidance can become ordinary capabilities inside interface architecture without forcing users into a conversational text box.</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong class="text-[var(--text-primary)] block mb-1">3. Abstraction Should Remove Complexity, Not Relocate It</strong>
            <span>Some of the most rewarding interface problems involve taking complicated underlying automation, backend mechanics, or technical systems and designing an interaction model that makes the capability substantially easier for a person to understand and control.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Career & Service -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--text-muted)] font-semibold mb-4">
          Career & Service
        </h2>
        <div class="prose max-w-none">
          <p>
            Throughout my career, I have led frontend and UI technical direction, established engineering standards, collaborated closely across design and engineering teams, and remained hands-on in the codebase.
          </p>
          <p>
            I served four years in the U.S. Navy from 2002 to 2006 as a Petty Officer (SH3). While serving, I independently designed and developed my ship's website outside my assigned duties, work that was recognized with a Navy Commendation Medal.
          </p>
          <p>
            After leaving the Navy, I studied Computer Science at William Paterson University and worked part-time as a web developer before moving into frontend engineering full-time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Creative & Adjacent Interests -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--text-muted)] font-semibold mb-4">
          Creative & Adjacent Interests
        </h2>
        <div class="prose max-w-none">
          <p>
            Outside of software, I'm especially interested in modular synthesis, gaming, and the places where physical and digital interfaces meet. MIDI, control surfaces, signal routing, and interactive systems are areas I find myself naturally drawn toward.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Identity & The Two Domains -->
      <section class="p-6 rounded-lg border border-[var(--border-subtle)] bg-[var(--bg-card)]">
        <h2 class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--text-muted)] font-semibold mb-3">
          Identity & The Two Domains
        </h2>
        <div class="prose max-w-none text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <p>
            <strong>orielsy.com</strong> is my canonical personal home on the web—housing my professional identity, technical writing, architecture explorations, and selected projects.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>bodyctrl.com</strong> is a name I've used around frontend and interactive work. The name is a small web-development joke: <code>&lt;body&gt; + Ctrl</code>—a frontend developer spends much of the craft controlling what happens inside the document body. I'm keeping BODYCTRL around as a home for experiments that naturally fit that identity, without forcing a hard separation from this site yet.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Connect & Links -->
      <section class="pt-6 border-t border-[var(--border-subtle)]">
        <h2 class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--text-muted)] font-semibold mb-4">
          Connect & Channels
        </h2>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 text-xs font-mono">
          <a
            href="https://github.com/orielsy"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="px-3.5 py-2 rounded border border-[var(--border-subtle)] bg-[var(--bg-card)] text-[var(--text-primary)] hover:border-[var(--border-strong)] flex items-center gap-2 transition-colors"
          >
            <span>GitHub (@orielsy)</span>
          </a>
          <a
            href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/orielsy"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="px-3.5 py-2 rounded border border-[var(--border-subtle)] bg-[var(--bg-card)] text-[var(--text-primary)] hover:border-[var(--border-strong)] flex items-center gap-2 transition-colors"
          >
            <span>LinkedIn</span>
          </a>
          <a
            href="https://bodyctrl.com"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="px-3.5 py-2 rounded border border-[var(--border-subtle)] bg-[var(--bg-card)] text-[var(--text-primary)] hover:border-[var(--border-strong)] flex items-center gap-2 transition-colors"
          >
            <span>bodyctrl.com</span>
          </a>
          <a
            href="/rss.xml"
            class="px-3.5 py-2 rounded bg-[var(--accent)] text-white hover:bg-[var(--accent-hover)] font-medium flex items-center gap-1.5 transition-colors"
          >
            <span>Subscribe via RSS</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
